The 3 Main Building Blocks

1. Browser
    Represents the actual browser instance (Chromium, Firefox, WebKit).

    Think of it as starting Chrome/Firefox on your computer.

    In Playwright, you can launch:

        browser = p.chromium.launch()
        browser = p.firefox.launch()
        browser = p.webkit.launch()

    Key options:
        headless=True → runs without showing the browser window.
        slow_mo=100 → slows actions for easier debugging.

2. Browser Context
    A separate browser profile within the same browser instance.
    Each context has its own cookies, cache, local storage — like incognito mode.
    Useful for running tests in parallel without interference.

    Example:
        context = browser.new_context()
    Note:
        You can create multiple contexts for different users/logins at the same time.

3. Page
    A single browser tab inside a context.
    This is where you interact with the site(click, type, extract data).

    Example:
        page = context.new_page()
        page.goto("https://example.com")

Flow of these concepts:
    Browser → Context → Page → Interactions


Example:

    from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright

    with sync_playwright() as p:
        browser = p.chromium.launch()
        context = browser.new_context()
        page = context.new_page()
        page.goto("https://example.com")
        print(f"Title: {page.title()}")
        browser.close()
